<Layouts.app flash={@flash}>
  <article id="about-page" class="lbm-about">
    <header class="lbm-hero lbm-about-hero">
      <p class="lbm-eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="lbm-hero-title">Making math less intimidating</h1>
      <p class="lbm-hero-lede">
        Load Bearing Math is a collection of short, hands-on walkthroughs of the math behind the
        systems we use every day: search, rate limiters, autoscalers, optimization, and more. The goal
        is simple: demystify math.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="about-mission" class="lbm-about-section">
      <p>
        Most math aversion isn't about ability. It's the overwhelming feeling of looking at a problem
        all at once. That feeling fades as the problem gets broken into smaller pieces, and once things
        start clicking, it's one of the most enjoyable feelings there is. It took until calculus for all
        of it to click for me, so if it hasn't clicked for you yet, you're in good company.
      </p>
      <p>
        So much of computer science, and of the technology around us, is built on mathematics. As
        software matured, a lot of that math got abstracted away, and the same thing is happening again
        with AI. It's worth remembering where these ideas come from, however technical you are.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about-who" class="lbm-about-section" aria-labelledby="about-who-heading">
      <h2 id="about-who-heading">Who's behind it</h2>
      <p>
        I'm Andrew Ferguson. I have a B.S. in Computer Science with a minor in Mathematics, and since
        graduating I've worked across a wide range of technical fields, including aerospace, space
        systems, cybersecurity, and large-scale systems integration. Nearly all of it runs on the kind of
        math you'll find on this site.
      </p>
      <p>
        Teaching has run alongside the engineering. I tutored math and science in college, and today I
        mentor through the SkillBridge program, helping service members sharpen their engineering
        skills as they move into industry.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also take on select consulting, advisory, and full-time engineering work.
        <.link navigate={~p"/work-with-me"} id="about-work-link">
          Work with me <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </.link>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="about-teaching"
      class="lbm-about-section"
      aria-labelledby="about-teaching-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="about-teaching-heading">How I teach</h2>
      <p>
        We work real problems, and as often as possible we look at where the math actually gets used.
        "You'll never use this" is one of the most common things said about math, and it's rarely
        true: cosine similarity is how search engines decide what counts as similar, and the leaky
        bucket is how servers keep from being overwhelmed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Students work through the problems themselves, with me as a guide, and we come back to the ideas
        later to reinforce them so they stick.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about-site" class="lbm-about-section" aria-labelledby="about-site-heading">
      <h2 id="about-site-heading">About the topics</h2>
      <p>
        Every topic here is worked out by hand. The worked examples sit behind a button so you can skip
        them, or click through and check the work. If you find a mistake, please tell me. And if there's
        something you'd like to see covered, use the Suggest a topic button at the top of the page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about-personal" class="lbm-about-section">
      <p class="lbm-about-personal">
        Outside of math, I'm based in New Hampshire and have lived all over New England. I like skiing, gaming, reading, and spending time with my dog, cat, and
        newborn.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="lbm-landing-hero-actions">
      <.button navigate={~p"/topics"} variant="primary" id="about-topics">
        Browse all topics <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
      </.button>
      <.button navigate={~p"/tutoring"} id="about-services">Tutoring services</.button>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layouts.app>
